BANK.push(
{"id":"o1","d":6,"q":"A national retailer's SIEM is licensed by daily ingest and now takes in 4 TB per day. About 70% of that volume is DNS, web proxy, and NetFlow data that analysts touch mainly during investigations; most detections run on EDR, identity, and firewall deny events. PCI DSS requires one year of audit log retention with the last three months immediately available. The SIEM vendor is shipping a new analyst UI next quarter. The CFO wants a 30% cost cut this fiscal year. Which design BEST meets the goal?","o":["Add a pipeline that filters DNS, proxy, and flow data into the SIEM and sends full copies to a searchable low-cost data lake","Move to a workload-based SIEM license so cost follows query compute instead of ingest volume, keeping all current sources in the hot tier","Stop sending raw DNS and proxy data and rely on the EDR's endpoint network telemetry for those investigations","Keep 90 days of all sources in the SIEM and roll older data to offline tape to meet the one-year rule"],"a":[0],"e":"A pipeline that routes bulky investigative data to a cheap, still-searchable tier cuts ingest cost while keeping detection data hot and meeting PCI retention. A license model change may help but leaves the 4 TB hot and is a procurement bet, not a design. Dropping DNS and proxy loses visibility for unmanaged devices that EDR does not see. The 90-day plus tape option meets PCI but barely touches ingest volume, which is the cost driver."},
{"id":"o2","d":6,"q":"An outside IR firm finished a ransomware investigation and found initial access through the VPN 47 days before encryption. The company kept endpoint telemetry for 14 days, firewall logs for 30, and VPN logs only on the appliance, which rolled over after 7. The firm could not say what data left the network, and the insurer is now questioning the claim. The board approved budget for logging improvements and asked the architect for a plan. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Raise EDR telemetry retention to 180 days, since endpoint data gave the most useful evidence in this case","Define per-source retention from dwell-time data, legal and insurance needs, and investigative use","Forward VPN appliance logs to the SIEM right away, since that source had the shortest retention","Engage an MDR provider whose service includes a year of telemetry retention for all integrated sources"],"a":[1],"e":"The gap was a missing retention requirement across sources, so the first step is to define how long each source must be kept and where, based on realistic dwell times and legal, insurance, and investigative needs. Extending EDR alone repeats the piecemeal approach. Forwarding VPN logs is a good quick fix but should follow from the requirements. An MDR retention offer covers only the sources it integrates and skips the requirements work."},
{"id":"o3","d":6,"q":"An eight-person SOC spends about 40% of its time on phishing triage, copying indicators between the email gateway, the EDR console, the IdP, and the SIEM. The EDR vendor is pitching its XDR bundle, but the email gateway is from a different vendor with a three-year contract. The company also plans to replace its SIEM in about 18 months. Analysts work from shared runbooks in a wiki. Which approach BEST reduces the triage workload over the next few years?","o":["Adopt the EDR vendor's XDR platform and connect the third-party email gateway through its partner integrations","Accelerate the SIEM replacement and pick a platform with built-in phishing analytics and case management","Deploy a vendor-neutral SOAR platform and build an automated phishing enrichment and response playbook as the first use case","Hire two more analysts and convert the wiki runbooks into a structured, checklist-based triage procedure"],"a":[2],"e":"A standalone SOAR tool orchestrates across vendors and survives the planned SIEM swap, and phishing is a high-volume, repeatable use case ideal for automation. XDR from the EDR vendor tends to work best within its own stack, and the email gateway is locked in for three years. Pulling the SIEM replacement forward is a large, risky project to solve a workflow problem. More staff and better checklists help but do not remove the manual copying."},
{"id":"o4","d":6,"q":"A SOAR playbook disables any account flagged for impossible travel. Last month a network team changed VPN egress IPs, and the playbook disabled the CEO and 300 field sales staff during quarter-end. Sales leadership wants all automated containment turned off. The SOC says manual handling would add hours to response for real account takeovers. The SOAR platform runs on-premises and is due for a version upgrade. What is the BEST redesign?","o":["Add the new VPN egress ranges to the playbook's trusted locations and require network change tickets to notify the SOC","Pause automated containment until the rule logic is rewritten and tested, handling the travel anomaly alerts manually","Require the user's manager to approve each automated disable through a chat prompt before the playbook runs","Tier actions by confidence and impact: auto-revoke sessions and force MFA here, and have analysts approve disables"],"a":[3],"e":"Matching the action to confidence and blast radius keeps fast automated response while reserving disruptive steps for human approval. Allowlisting the egress IPs fixes this trigger but not the design flaw. Pausing automation gives up the speed benefit the SOC needs. Manager approval puts a non-security person in the loop and stalls response at night and on weekends."},
{"id":"o5","d":6,"q":"An order management system has a BIA-approved RTO of 4 hours and RPO of 1 hour. The database ships transaction logs to a DR region every 15 minutes. Nightly full backups go to immutable storage. In DR, the application tier is rebuilt from infrastructure-as-code, which took 5 hours in the last test because of image builds and manual DNS changes. The DR region uses a different instance family than production. Which statement is MOST accurate?","o":["The design meets both targets, because immutable nightly backups give a clean recovery point inside the window","The RPO is met through log shipping, but the RTO is missed because the application tier takes 5 hours to rebuild","The RPO is missed because nightly backups set the recovery point, but the RTO is met by the replicated database","The RTO is met for data since the database is already in the DR region, and the app tier is outside the BIA target"],"a":[1],"e":"Log shipping every 15 minutes keeps data loss under the 1-hour RPO, but the service is not back until the app tier is up, and that took 5 hours, over the 4-hour RTO. Immutable backups matter for ransomware but do not set the RPO here. The nightly backup is not the primary recovery point when logs are shipped. RTO applies to the service, not just the database."},
{"id":"o6","d":6,"q":"Payroll has a maximum tolerable downtime of 12 hours. IT can restore the payroll platform in 8 hours. After restore, payroll staff need about 5 hours to re-enter and reconcile transactions lost since the last recovery point. A vendor offers a premium tier that cuts restore to 6 hours for a higher annual fee. Payroll runs every two weeks. Which statement is MOST accurate?","o":["The plan is acceptable, since the 8-hour restore is well inside the 12-hour MTD","The MTD should be revised to 13 hours to match the tested recovery capability","The plan overshoots MTD by an hour; a faster restore or less rework would fix it","Work recovery time is a business activity and is planned separately from IT recovery"],"a":[2],"e":"RTO plus work recovery time must fit within MTD. Here 8 plus 5 is 13 hours, one over the limit. Either the premium restore tier or a tighter RPO that reduces rework closes the gap. RTO alone being under MTD is not enough. MTD comes from business impact and should not be stretched to fit IT capability. WRT is planned with the business but still counts against MTD."},
{"id":"o7","d":6,"q":"A payments platform needs an RTO of 15 minutes and near-zero data loss. The regulator requires a recovery site at least 150 miles from the primary. The database is licensed per core, and the budget allows one extra full-size copy plus a small one. The application is latency sensitive, with sub-5 ms write commit targets. Staff are split across two offices in the same metro. Which design BEST meets these requirements?","o":["Synchronous replication to a nearby metro site for zero loss, plus asynchronous replication to a distant warm standby site","Synchronous replication straight to a hot site 150 miles away so that each committed write exists in both places","Active-active across two distant regions with asynchronous replication and application-level conflict resolution","A cloud pilot-light environment in a distant region with continuous asynchronous replication and automated scale-up"],"a":[0],"e":"Synchronous replication over 150 miles adds round-trip latency that breaks the 5 ms commit target, so a metro synchronous copy gives near-zero RPO and fast failover while an async distant copy meets the regulator. Active-active across distant regions costs more cores than budgeted and risks conflicts. Pilot light usually cannot meet a 15-minute RTO and leaves some data loss."},
{"id":"o8","d":6,"q":"A regional bank ran a DR tabletop last year and a checklist review this spring. It has never actually brought up systems at the DR site. The regulator now expects evidence that core banking can run from DR, but the business will not accept any planned interruption to production during the review period. The DR site was refreshed with new storage six months ago. Which test should the bank run NEXT?","o":["A full interruption test during a low-volume weekend, cutting production over to the DR site","Another tabletop with technical injects that walk through the new storage failover steps","A parallel test that brings DR systems up and processes real transactions without cutting over","A component test of the new storage replication alone, with results documented for the regulator"],"a":[2],"e":"A parallel test proves DR systems can run real workloads without touching production, which fits both the regulator and the no-interruption constraint. A full interruption test is the most realistic but the business ruled it out. Another tabletop does not show systems actually work. A storage component test is useful but does not show core banking runs from DR."},
{"id":"o9","d":6,"q":"A retailer runs stores and e-commerce around the clock and has budget for six SOC analysts. A union agreement limits how many night shifts each analyst can work. The environment includes a custom point-of-sale platform whose logs an MDR provider cannot parse without custom work. The CISO wants after-hours coverage within six months. The SOC recently moved to a new office. Which operating model is MOST appropriate?","o":["Staff a 24x7 internal rotation using the six analysts on 12-hour shifts, with on-call backup for nights","Outsource all monitoring to an MSSP, including custom parsing of the point-of-sale logs","Use MDR for after-hours triage of standard telemetry, with the internal team owning POS detections","Run a follow-the-sun model with a partner SOC in another time zone handling nights"],"a":[2],"e":"Six analysts cannot sustainably cover 24x7, especially under night-shift limits, so a hybrid model gives coverage while keeping internal ownership of the custom POS detections the provider cannot handle. A six-person rotation burns out staff. Full outsourcing hands off detections the provider is weak at. A follow-the-sun partner is close to MDR but adds coordination cost without solving the POS gap."},
{"id":"o10","d":6,"q":"A SOC reports 80% MITRE ATT&CK coverage based on how its detection rules are tagged. In a recent red team exercise that mimicked a ransomware group active in the sector, the SOC caught only two of seven attack stages. Leadership asks why the coverage number did not reflect reality. The SOC has a breach and attack simulation license that is mostly unused. What should the architect do NEXT?","o":["Buy a commercial detection content pack tagged to the techniques that currently show no coverage","Re-map existing rules at the sub-technique level so the coverage report reflects real precision","Run BAS tests across the whole ATT&CK matrix and use the pass rate as the new coverage metric","Run purple-team emulation of that group's techniques and measure which of the SOC's detections actually fire"],"a":[3],"e":"Tag-based coverage measures intent, not effectiveness. Emulating the techniques of the most relevant threat and checking what actually fires shows real gaps and lets the team fix them. More content adds tags without proof. Sub-technique re-mapping refines the same paper metric. Running BAS across the full matrix is broad but not prioritized by threat, and pass rate on generic tests can again overstate readiness."},
{"id":"o11","d":6,"q":"A company subscribes to 30 threat feeds that deliver millions of indicators, and analysts are swamped by matches on stale or low-value IOCs. The CTI team has two people. Leadership wants to add more feeds after a peer was breached. Business units have never been asked what intelligence they need. A threat intelligence platform is in the budget for next year. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Deploy the threat intelligence platform early to deduplicate, score, and expire indicators across feeds","Define intelligence requirements with stakeholders, then select and tune feeds to answer them","Automatically block high-confidence indicators at the firewall to cut alert volume","Reduce sources to government and ISAC feeds, which are typically better vetted than commercial ones"],"a":[1],"e":"Without defined requirements there is no basis for choosing feeds, scoring, or aging indicators, so requirements come first. A TIP helps manage volume but needs requirements to tune it. Auto-blocking cuts alerts but can block legitimate traffic and does not fix relevance. Cutting to ISAC feeds is a guess that may drop the intelligence that matters most."},
{"id":"o12","d":6,"q":"Two companies merged six months ago and still run separate IR teams, legal departments, and ticketing systems. During a recent phishing incident affecting both, each team waited for the other to approve isolating shared servers, and containment slipped by a day. The integration office wants one IR plan this quarter. Both SOCs use the same EDR product. Which element of the plan should be defined FIRST?","o":["A merged severity matrix so that both teams rate incidents the same way","Joint playbooks for the most common scenarios, starting with phishing","Roles, responsibilities, and who has authority to declare and contain","A single ticketing and case management system for both IR teams"],"a":[2],"e":"The delay came from unclear authority, so the plan must first say who leads, who decides, and who can authorize containment across both companies. A shared severity matrix, joint playbooks, and one case system all matter but depend on knowing who owns the decisions."},
{"id":"o13","d":6,"q":"An attacker has a foothold on an engineering file server and is actively staging archives of export-controlled designs for outbound transfer. Federal agents involved in a broader investigation ask the company to keep monitoring quietly for a few more weeks. The legal team is concerned about export control violations. The server runs an older OS that is due for replacement. What should the incident lead do?","o":["Keep monitoring at the agents' request and record all activity to support the broader case","Isolate and rebuild the server immediately and reset all engineering credentials the same day","Block the exfil path and protect the data, keep watching other footholds, and get legal and agency sign-off","Move the designs to a new share and leave decoy files in place to track the attacker's next moves"],"a":[2],"e":"The deciding factor is ongoing harm against intelligence value. Active staging of export-controlled data is serious harm, so the exfil path must be stopped, while monitoring can continue where it does not risk the data, with legal and law enforcement coordination. Pure monitoring accepts the loss. An immediate rebuild may tip off the attacker before the full scope is known. Moving files and planting decoys is useful deception but leaves the exfil channel open."},
{"id":"o14","d":6,"q":"A Linux server in a colocation facility is suspected of running a kernel rootkit. The responder has remote shell access only, and the system owner wants to reboot it within the hour to restore service. Forensic analysis may support a lawsuit. The server also forwards syslog to a central SIEM, and the colocation provider can supply console access if needed. Which collection sequence is BEST?","o":["Run the server's own netstat and ps to record connections and processes, then capture memory, then image the disk","Capture memory with trusted tools from external media, then network state, then a disk image, then remote logs","Pull SIEM logs first to scope the incident, then capture memory and network state, then shut down and image the disk","Shut down cleanly to protect file system consistency, then image the disk with a write blocker, then pull logs"],"a":[1],"e":"Memory is the most volatile and holds rootkit artifacts, and it must be captured with trusted tools since a rootkit can hide from local binaries. Disk follows, and remote logs are least volatile and can wait. Running local netstat and ps relies on tools the rootkit may control and changes memory first. Starting with SIEM logs wastes the volatile window. A clean shutdown destroys memory evidence."},
{"id":"o15","d":6,"q":"A company suspects a departing engineer stole source code. An IT admin copied files from the engineer's laptop to a USB drive and emailed screenshots to HR. Counsel now says the evidence may be challenged in court. The company uses Microsoft 365 and a commercial EDR. Leadership asks what the forensic readiness program should focus on to avoid this outcome in future. What is MOST important?","o":["Automated legal hold in Microsoft 365 so that mailboxes and OneDrive content are preserved on demand","Documented procedures with chain of custody, hashing at acquisition, and trained staff using validated tools","A year of full EDR telemetry retention so that file access evidence exists whenever an employee departs","A data loss prevention policy that blocks source code uploads to personal cloud storage, email, and removable media"],"a":[1],"e":"Admissibility depends on showing evidence was collected properly and not altered: chain of custody, hashes taken at acquisition, and repeatable methods by trained people. Legal hold preserves data but does not make ad hoc collection defensible. Longer EDR retention gives more data but not defensible handling. DLP is prevention, not evidence handling."},
{"id":"o16","d":6,"q":"During an investigation, analysts could not line up events from cloud services, which log in UTC, with on-premises firewalls that log local time without a timezone field. The SIEM stamped those firewall events with ingest time, and some arrived hours late after a collector backlog. One domain controller had also drifted four minutes, which surfaced as Kerberos errors. The SOC recently upgraded its SIEM storage. Which design change BEST fixes the root problem?","o":["Configure the SIEM to use ingest time for all sources so each event follows one consistent clock","Point all devices at a public NTP pool and add per-source timezone offsets in the SIEM parsers","Use an authenticated internal NTP hierarchy, log in UTC with offsets, and keep both event and ingest time","Increase collector capacity to clear the backlog and alert when a domain controller drifts beyond one minute"],"a":[2],"e":"Reliable timelines need trusted, synchronized clocks, timestamps with explicit timezones, and both event time and ingest time so delays are visible. Ingest time hides delays like the backlog seen here. A public pool lacks authentication and parser offsets break on daylight saving changes. More collector capacity and drift alerts treat symptoms."},
{"id":"o17","d":6,"q":"A company's scanners report 200,000 open findings, 30,000 of them CVSS 9 or higher. Remediation teams work strictly by CVSS score and cannot keep up. Last quarter an attacker exploited a medium-severity CVE on an internet-facing VPN appliance that was on the CISA KEV list. The asset inventory has no business criticality or exposure tags. The scanning tool's license renews next month. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Set a 15-day SLA for all CVSS 9+ findings and report team compliance to the executive committee","Tag assets for exposure and criticality, then rank open findings by KEV listing, EPSS score, and those tags","Replace the scanner at renewal with a platform that includes built-in risk-based scoring","Move from monthly to weekly scanning so newly exploited vulnerabilities are found sooner"],"a":[1],"e":"Risk-based prioritization needs exploit likelihood (KEV, EPSS) plus asset exposure and criticality, and the inventory gap blocks that, so tagging comes first. A tighter CVSS SLA keeps the wrong ranking and would have missed the exploited medium CVE. A new tool still needs asset context. Faster scans add findings without better triage."},
{"id":"o18","d":6,"q":"A hospital's change policy requires 30 days of testing for every patch. A critical remote code execution flaw in its remote access gateway is being actively exploited, and the vendor has released both a patch and a configuration mitigation. Clinicians rely on the gateway for after-hours access to imaging systems. The change advisory board meets weekly, with the next meeting in six days. What should happen?","o":["Wait for the next CAB meeting so the patch can enter testing through the normal approval process","Take the gateway offline until the patch completes its 30-day testing cycle to remove all exposure","Use the emergency change path: apply the mitigation now, hunt for compromise, then fast-track the patch","Apply the vendor patch to production tonight, skipping testing, because the exposure is actively exploited"],"a":[2],"e":"An emergency path lets the hospital cut risk at once with the vendor mitigation, check whether it was already compromised, and patch on an accelerated test cycle. Waiting six days leaves an exploited flaw open. Taking the gateway offline for a month hurts clinical care. Patching untested in production may be justified in some cases, but here a safer mitigation exists and the hunt is missing."},
{"id":"o19","d":6,"q":"An outage was traced to an undocumented firewall rule a contractor added through a shared admin account. The network team has an ITSM tool, nightly configuration backups, and TACACS+ command accounting that nobody reviews. Management wants to catch unauthorized changes quickly without slowing approved work. The firewall pair is due for a hardware refresh next year. Which control BEST detects such changes?","o":["Require dual approval in the ITSM tool for firewall changes before engineers can start the work","Email nightly config diffs to the network team so they can spot unexpected changes","Detect config drift against the approved baseline and reconcile each change to a ticket","Review TACACS+ command logs each month and flag commands run from shared accounts"],"a":[2],"e":"Comparing actual configuration to a baseline and matching each change to an approved ticket catches unauthorized changes regardless of how they were made. Dual approval is a preventive control that this contractor bypassed. Emailed diffs show changes but rely on people to judge which are approved. Monthly log review is slow, and shared accounts hide who acted."},
{"id":"o20","d":6,"q":"In a recent incident, attackers with domain admin rights loaded a vulnerable signed driver to kill the EDR agent on 40 servers, then deployed ransomware. Tamper protection was already enabled. The EDR console showed the servers as offline for two hours, and analysts assumed it was patching. The servers run a mix of Windows Server 2019 and 2022. Which improvement MOST directly addresses this attack?","o":["Turn on the EDR product's tamper protection policy for all server groups and lock its settings","Alert when agents go silent unexpectedly, correlated with other telemetry, and block vulnerable drivers","Deploy a second EDR product on critical servers so that one agent can still cover if the other is disabled","Remove local admin rights from server operators and require just-in-time elevation through PAM"],"a":[1],"e":"The attack used a driver to bypass tamper protection, and the silence went unnoticed. Alerting on unexpected agent loss and blocking known vulnerable drivers both target this path. Tamper protection was already on. A second EDR adds cost and the same driver can kill both. Removing local admin is good hygiene but the attacker already had domain admin."},
{"id":"o21","d":6,"q":"A company has 120 AWS accounts in an organization. Account owners enabled CloudTrail inconsistently, and logs sit in buckets inside each account. GuardDuty runs in two accounts. A recent review found that an admin in any member account can stop logging and delete its trail data. The SOC wants solid detection and investigation of control-plane activity. Most workloads run in two regions. What should be done FIRST?","o":["Enable GuardDuty across the organization with a delegated administrator account","Create an organization trail for all regions writing to a locked-down, separate log archive account","Enable VPC flow logs in all accounts and forward them to the SIEM for analysis","Deploy a CSPM tool to flag accounts where CloudTrail is disabled or configured incorrectly"],"a":[1],"e":"Control-plane detection and forensics depend on complete API audit logs that a compromised member account cannot tamper with, so an org-wide trail to a separate archive account comes first. GuardDuty is valuable but does not give the SOC a retained, protected audit log. Flow logs show network traffic, not API calls. CSPM reports the gap without fixing log integrity."},
{"id":"o22","d":6,"q":"An R&D firm with sites in Germany and the US wants to detect engineers taking designs before they leave. DLP already produces hundreds of daily alerts that analysts mostly ignore. HR knows about resignations days or weeks before last day. The German works council requires monitoring to be proportionate and agreed in advance. The firm uses a SaaS file platform. Which approach is MOST effective within these constraints?","o":["Log keystrokes and screens for all R&D staff to capture exfiltration attempts in full detail","Block USB storage and personal cloud uploads for all staff, and review DLP alerts more often","Feed HR departure signals into UEBA for risk-based monitoring, as agreed with the works council","Have managers review weekly file access reports for staff who have given notice of their departure"],"a":[2],"e":"UEBA with HR context focuses attention on the people and periods with real risk, cuts noise, and can be scoped and pseudonymized to meet works council rules. Keystroke and screen capture for everyone is disproportionate. Blocking helps prevention but the DLP noise problem stays. Manager reviews are slow and inconsistent."},
{"id":"o23","d":6,"q":"A company's IR plan assumes the team will coordinate in Microsoft Teams and email. In a tabletop exercise the scenario included an attacker with Global Admin in Entra ID, and the team realized it could not trust any tool tied to that tenant. Some responders use corporate iPhones enrolled in Intune. Legal requires that incident communications be retained. What should the IR architecture include?","o":["A private Teams channel limited to IR members, with retention policies set by legal","A pre-built channel outside corporate identity, with offline contact lists and retention configured","Personal mobile numbers for all responders, kept in a shared contact sheet in SharePoint","Satellite phones for the IR lead and CISO so leadership can coordinate during an outage"],"a":[1],"e":"Out-of-band communication has to sit outside the compromised identity plane, be set up before an incident, and meet retention rules. A private Teams channel still depends on the compromised tenant. Personal numbers in SharePoint rely on the same tenant and lack retention. Satellite phones cover only two people and ignore records."},
{"id":"o24","d":6,"q":"A mid-size firm with a three-person SOC wants high-fidelity detection of credential theft and lateral movement in Active Directory. Its existing honeypot appliance fires daily, mostly from the internal vulnerability scanner and IT discovery tools. The team has little time for tuning. The firm also plans to move file shares to SharePoint next year. Which deception design gives the BEST signal with the least effort?","o":["Deploy several high-interaction honeypots on internal subnets to observe attacker behavior","Plant fake privileged credentials and decoy accounts on endpoints, alerting on their use","Build a honeynet in the DMZ to collect attacker tools and techniques for threat intel","Exclude the scanner and discovery tools on the current honeypot and keep it running"],"a":[1],"e":"Honeytoken credentials have no legitimate use, so any use is a strong signal of theft or lateral movement, and they need little upkeep. High-interaction honeypots need care and maintenance a small team lacks. A DMZ honeynet gathers external intel, not internal lateral movement. Tuning the current appliance helps but network decoys still draw noise from legitimate tools."},
{"id":"o25","d":6,"q":"A SOC reports alerts closed per analyst and tickets handled per day to the executive committee. After a breach that went undetected for weeks, the board asks for metrics that show whether the SOC limits attacker impact. The SOC uses an MDR provider at night and a ticketing system that timestamps every state change. Which pair of metrics BEST answers the board?","o":["Share of alerts closed within SLA, and analyst utilization across all shifts","Time to detect and time to contain, measured from first attacker action","Number of blocked threats per month, and phishing simulation click rate","Detection true-positive rate, and percentage of ATT&CK techniques covered"],"a":[1],"e":"Limiting impact depends on how fast the SOC finds and stops attackers, measured from when the attack actually started, not from when an alert fired. SLA closure and utilization measure throughput. Blocked threats and click rates measure prevention and awareness. True-positive rate and coverage matter for tuning but do not show impact."},
{"id":"o26","d":6,"q":"A manufacturer's SOC monitors only IT. The plant-floor OT network has no monitoring, and OT engineers worry that agents or active scans could trip safety systems or stop lines. Several HMIs run unsupported Windows versions certified by the machine vendor. Managed switches at the supervisory level support SPAN. The board wants OT threat visibility after a peer's ransomware reached its plants. What is the MOST appropriate approach?","o":["Install EDR agents on engineering workstations and HMIs during the next planned maintenance window","Run an active asset discovery scan during a shutdown to build the OT inventory before monitoring","Add passive ICS-aware network monitoring on SPAN ports feeding the SOC, with OT playbooks","Put a firewall between IT and OT first, then decide on monitoring after segmentation"],"a":[2],"e":"Passive ICS-aware monitoring gives visibility without touching fragile systems, uses the SPAN capability that exists, and joint playbooks bring OT engineers into response. EDR on vendor-certified HMIs can break support and stability. An active scan during shutdown is possible but does not give ongoing visibility. Segmentation is important but the board asked for visibility now."},
{"id":"o27","d":6,"q":"A financial firm's IR plan says the general counsel must approve any notification to regulators. During a weekend incident, the general counsel was unreachable for 36 hours while traveling, and the firm missed a 24-hour reporting deadline. The deputy general counsel was available but felt she lacked authority. The firm also uses an outside law firm on retainer. What is the BEST fix?","o":["Add the general counsel's personal contacts and a 24x7 escalation path to the IR call tree","Give the outside law firm authority to submit regulatory notices when internal counsel is unavailable","Define delegated authority and named alternates for key roles, with pre-approved notice criteria","Automate regulator notification when an incident reaches the severity threshold in the case system"],"a":[2],"e":"The failure was a single point of authority. Named alternates with explicit delegation, plus clear criteria, let the deputy act. Better contact details still leave one person as the bottleneck. Outside counsel advising is fine, but the decision belongs to the firm. Automated notices remove judgment from a legal decision."},
{"id":"o28","d":6,"q":"A company runs its own data center and is building a continuity plan for a pandemic where up to 40% of staff may be out for weeks. Some tasks need people on site, such as tape rotation and hardware swaps. Three senior engineers hold most of the knowledge about the storage platform. The VPN concentrator is already near capacity. The data center is co-located with a regional office. Which planning element is MOST important?","o":["Buy additional VPN licenses and bandwidth so that most staff can work remotely for long periods","Contract remote-hands services from a third party for on-site tasks such as tape and hardware work","Map critical roles and knowledge gaps, cross-train and document, and set minimum on-site staffing","Start moving workloads to cloud so that the dependence on on-site data center staff is reduced"],"a":[2],"e":"A people-focused disruption is mainly a staffing and knowledge problem, so mapping critical roles, removing single points of knowledge, and defining minimum on-site staffing comes first. More VPN capacity and remote hands are useful parts of the plan but do not fix the knowledge risk. Moving to cloud is a long-term strategy, not a continuity plan."},
{"id":"o29","d":6,"q":"A company needs Windows event logs and EDR telemetry from 30,000 laptops, about 70% of which are rarely on the corporate network. Today collection uses Windows Event Forwarding over the VPN, which uses split tunneling. Some users are in countries with metered bandwidth. The privacy office wants collection limited to what security needs. The laptops are refreshed every four years. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Switch to a persistent full-tunnel VPN so that Windows Event Forwarding can reach all of the laptops","Collect only when laptops connect to the VPN, and accept the gaps for remote periods","Rely on EDR telemetry alone and stop collecting Windows event logs from laptops","Use agents that send filtered events to a cloud collector over mutual TLS, buffering offline"],"a":[3],"e":"Internet-facing collection with mutual TLS, local buffering, and agent-side filtering works on any network, limits bandwidth, and supports privacy. Full-tunnel VPN adds load and user impact just for logging. Collecting only on VPN leaves big blind spots. EDR alone misses some OS events investigators rely on."},
{"id":"o30","d":6,"q":"Attackers in a recent breach used stolen but valid domain credentials, and signature-based SIEM rules did not fire. The AD environment has 15,000 users, a mix of service and admin accounts, and a three-year-old tiering project that stalled. The SOC has EDR on endpoints and domain controller logs in the SIEM. Which detection approach is MOST effective against this attack pattern?","o":["Enforce MFA on all interactive domain logons so that stolen passwords alone cannot be used","Alert when failed logons for an account exceed a threshold within a short time window","Baseline logon behavior per account and host, alert on anomalies, and add decoy accounts","Restart the admin tiering project so privileged accounts cannot log on to lower-tier systems"],"a":[2],"e":"Valid credentials do not trigger failure-based rules, so detection must look for unusual behavior for that account, such as new hosts, logon types, or times, and decoy accounts give high-fidelity alerts. MFA and tiering are strong preventive controls, but the question asks for detection of credential misuse. Failed logon thresholds miss successful use of valid credentials."},
{"id":"o31","d":6,"q":"A company's backup dashboard showed green for every job for two years. During a ransomware recovery, restores of three key systems failed because the backup catalog was corrupted, and rebuilding it took two days. Auditors now want evidence that recovery works. Backups are written to a deduplicating appliance from the same vendor as the backup software. The finance team is asking for a cheaper storage tier. What is the BEST improvement?","o":["Copy backups to a second appliance from a different vendor so a single product flaw cannot hit both copies","Replicate the backup catalog to a separate system and alert on catalog consistency check failures","Schedule regular full restores of critical systems, verify the app works, and track results against RTO","Alert on job warnings as well as failures so that partial or skipped backups get reviewed by staff"],"a":[2],"e":"Only real restores, including bringing the application up, prove backups can meet recovery targets and give auditors evidence. A second vendor copy improves resilience but still is not tested. Protecting the catalog fixes this one failure cause but not others. Better job alerting still only measures backup completion, not recoverability."},
{"id":"o32","d":6,"q":"A SOC manager is proposing a threat hunting program to leadership. The team has strong EDR and SIEM telemetry, a small CTI function, and a backlog of low-priority alerts. Leadership asks which plan would add value beyond the existing automated detection. The SOC also plans to adopt a new case management tool next quarter, and two analysts have hunting training from a recent course. Which plan is BEST?","o":["Have analysts work through the lowest-priority alert queue each day to find threats that were missed","Sweep the environment weekly for indicators from new threat feeds and report any matches found","Run hypothesis-driven hunts based on intel and ATT&CK, and turn what they find into new detections","Run authenticated vulnerability scans of crown-jewel systems monthly and review the exposure"],"a":[2],"e":"Hunting is a proactive, hypothesis-driven search for activity that evaded detection, and its value compounds when findings become new automated detections. Working low-priority alerts is triage. IOC sweeps are a narrow form of hunting that finds only known indicators. Vulnerability scanning is exposure management, not hunting."},
{"id":"o33","d":6,"q":"A manufacturer has subsidiaries in the EU, China, and Brazil and a global SOC in the US. Chinese data rules restrict exporting certain personal and important data, and the EU subsidiary's works council has raised concerns about monitoring. The company can afford one SIEM vendor. The SOC wants to correlate attacks that span regions. Most attacks seen so far target the US business. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Centralize all logs in a US-hosted SIEM and use standard contractual clauses to cover the EU transfers","Run separate SIEMs and regional SOCs in each jurisdiction with no shared data between them","Pseudonymize all user fields before shipping logs to the US SIEM so that no personal data leaves","Keep logs in-region on one platform with federated search, sharing only allowed metadata centrally"],"a":[3],"e":"Regional storage on one platform keeps data where law requires while federated search and permitted metadata let the global SOC correlate. A US-only SIEM may cover EU transfers with SCCs but not Chinese export rules. Fully separate SOCs lose cross-region correlation and cost more. Pseudonymizing every field hurts investigations and may not satisfy rules on important data."},
{"id":"o34","d":6,"q":"After a major incident, an executive asks that the post-incident review name the administrator whose misconfiguration allowed initial access so HR can take action. Legal expects litigation and wants the review protected where possible. The IR firm has offered to write the full report. The company plans to run the review in its new collaboration tool. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Name the individual in the review so that accountability is clear and the same mistake is not repeated","Hold a blameless root cause review under counsel's direction and track each fix through to completion","Delay the review until the litigation is resolved so that nothing written can be used against the firm","Have the outside IR firm write the review alone so that internal politics do not shape the findings"],"a":[1],"e":"A blameless review focused on systemic causes gets honest input, and running it under counsel where appropriate helps with the litigation concern. Tracked actions make it lead to change. Naming one person discourages candor and misses system failures. Waiting for litigation loses lessons while memories fade. The IR firm can contribute but internal owners must accept and act on findings."},
{"id":"o35","d":6,"q":"A company's SIEM, SOAR, log collectors, and security jump hosts all run in the primary data center. The DR plan fails business apps over to a secondary site with a 4-hour RTO, but security tools are not in scope. In a ransomware tabletop, the team realized the SOC would be blind during the event it most needs to see. An MDR provider monitors nights using the on-premises collectors. Which design consideration is MOST important?","o":["Add the security tools to the DR runbook with the same 4-hour RTO as the business applications","Cluster the SIEM and SOAR inside the primary data center so a single server failure is tolerated","Rely on the MDR provider's own console for visibility while the primary data center is down","Host the monitoring and response tooling outside the failure domain that it is meant to watch"],"a":[3],"e":"Security tooling must stay up when the monitored environment fails, so it belongs outside that failure domain, for example in cloud or a separate site with independent identity. Adding it to DR still leaves the SOC blind for hours. Local clustering does not survive a site or ransomware event. The MDR depends on the same on-premises collectors."},
{"id":"o36","d":6,"q":"A leaked AWS access key belonging to the CI/CD pipeline was used to create two IAM users with access keys and a role that trusts an external AWS account. The pipeline deploys to production every hour, and the release manager warns that breaking it will delay a launch. CloudTrail is enabled organization-wide. The account also runs a legacy batch job nobody owns. Which action should be taken FIRST?","o":["Delete the attacker's IAM users and role right away to remove persistence, then investigate","Rotate the pipeline key, update the pipeline, and then review CloudTrail for other activity","Deactivate the leaked and attacker keys, block the new users and role, and revoke active sessions","Apply an SCP that denies all actions in the account until the investigation is complete"],"a":[2],"e":"Containment should cut off every attacker access path, including the external role trust, while keeping evidence intact, so deactivate and block rather than delete. Deleting destroys artifacts investigators need. Rotating only the pipeline key leaves the attacker's own users and role working. A deny-all SCP stops the attacker but also stops production, which is more disruption than needed when targeted actions work."},
{"id":"o37","d":6,"q":"A bug bounty researcher reports a SQL injection flaw in the customer portal, and the triage team confirms it. Development says a fix will ship in the next sprint, three weeks away. Code history shows the flaw has existed for 18 months. The portal sits behind a WAF in monitoring mode. The researcher has asked about a bonus for a fast report. What should the vulnerability handling process ensure NEXT?","o":["Notify all portal customers now that their account data may have been exposed through the flaw","Ask the researcher to retest after the sprint and pay the bounty once the fix is verified","Enforce a WAF rule now, search old logs for exploitation, and ship the fix within the SLA","Raise the fix to the top of the next sprint and pay the bounty based on the program terms"],"a":[2],"e":"A confirmed critical flaw needs quick risk reduction (a blocking WAF rule), a look back for past exploitation given 18 months of exposure, and a fix within the critical SLA. Notifying customers before knowing whether data was accessed is premature. Retest and payment are program steps, not risk handling. Reprioritizing the sprint leaves three weeks of exposure and skips the exploitation check."},
{"id":"o38","d":6,"q":"A company's IR plan has never been tested. The regulator has asked for evidence of testing within the year. The CEO wants an unannounced red team against production next month to see how the team really performs. Several playbooks were written by a consultant who has left, and two key responders joined last month. What should the architect recommend FIRST?","o":["Run the unannounced red team in production next month, as the CEO asked, for a realistic result","Do a tabletop with responders and executives first, then technical drills, then live exercises later","Have each team lead review the IR plan document and sign off that their section is accurate","Run a full simulated attack in production with all teams pre-briefed on the date and the scope"],"a":[1],"e":"An untested plan with new staff should start with a low-risk tabletop that exposes gaps in roles and decisions, then progress to technical and live tests. An unannounced red team now would mostly confirm the plan is untested, at higher risk. A document review alone does not test anything. A full production simulation is a later stage once basics are proven."},
{"id":"o39","d":6,"q":"A trading firm's application audit logs may be used in litigation. They are written locally on Linux servers where several admins have root. The SIEM receives a copy, but SIEM admins belong to the same team and can delete indexes. Counsel wants the logs to stand up to tampering claims. The firm is also moving to a new SIEM vendor next year. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Restrict root on the servers through PAM with session recording and approval workflows for sudo","Tighten SIEM role-based access so that only the SIEM team lead can delete indexed log data","Hash the log files daily and store the hashes in a separate directory on the same server","Forward logs in near real time to a WORM store in a separate account with hash checks"],"a":[3],"e":"Shipping logs quickly to write-once storage outside the admins' control, with hashing or chaining, makes tampering detectable and hard. PAM on root helps but local logs remain editable. SIEM RBAC still leaves one admin able to delete. Hashes kept on the same server can be changed by the same root user."},
{"id":"o40","d":6,"q":"Analysts receive about 5,000 alerts per day, and 95% are false positives. Some analysts have started bulk-closing entire rules. The SOC manager wants to buy an ML-based alert triage product, and a vendor has offered a free pilot. Detection rules have no owners or performance tracking. The SIEM was upgraded last quarter, and its license allows unlimited rules. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Pilot the ML triage product to rank alerts and auto-close those it scores as likely benign","Hire more tier-1 analysts to work the queue down while the detection rules are reviewed over time","Raise severity thresholds so analysts see only high and critical alerts on their dashboards","Track precision per rule, tune or retire weak rules, correlate alerts, and add asset context"],"a":[3],"e":"The root cause is unmanaged detection content. Measuring each rule, tuning or retiring weak ones, and correlating and enriching alerts fixes the source. ML triage can help later but hides bad rules and needs good inputs. More staff absorbs noise without reducing it. Higher thresholds hide true positives that sit in medium severity."},
{"id":"o41","d":6,"q":"A company runs an annual penetration test and a red team every two years. Firewall policies, EDR settings, and SIEM rules change weekly, and twice last year a change quietly broke detection for months. Leadership wants ongoing assurance that controls still detect and block common techniques after changes. The team uses a GRC tool for control attestations. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Move the penetration test to a quarterly schedule and include detection checks","Scan all systems weekly with authenticated vulnerability scans and track drift","Use breach and attack simulation on a schedule and check that alerts actually fire","Run an annual red team and purple team pair that produces a detection gap report each time"],"a":[2],"e":"BAS runs safe, repeatable attack simulations continuously or after changes and confirms detections and blocks still work, which is exactly the regression problem described. Quarterly pentests and annual red teams are valuable but too infrequent. Vulnerability scans find weaknesses, not detection failures."},
{"id":"o42","d":6,"q":"A Linux VM in an Azure scale set shows signs of compromise. The scale set may remove the instance during the next scale-in, which usually happens overnight. The app team wants to delete it and redeploy from a clean image. Legal says the evidence may be needed. The workload processes customer orders and has two other healthy instances. Which approach preserves evidence BEST?","o":["Delete and redeploy from a clean image, keeping the platform logs as the evidence record","Stop the VM to freeze its current state, then snapshot the OS and data disks and hash the snapshots","Protect it from scale-in, capture memory, snapshot disks, isolate it with an NSG, and log custody","Copy the application and system logs to a storage account, then let the scale set remove it"],"a":[2],"e":"Scale-in protection stops the platform from destroying the evidence, memory is captured before anything else, disks are snapshotted, and network isolation contains the attacker while keeping the VM running. Deleting or letting it scale in loses disk and memory. Stopping the VM loses memory before it is captured."},
{"id":"o43","d":6,"q":"A company rates incident severity by the CVSS score of the exploited vulnerability and the number of affected hosts. Last month a single compromised server in payroll was rated low and sat untouched for three days, while a noisy adware outbreak on 200 kiosks got top priority. The company is regulated and holds employee banking data. The SOC also wants to automate severity scoring. Which criteria should drive severity?","o":["Number of affected hosts and total alert volume, weighted by the analyst's confidence","CVSS of the exploited flaw, adjusted up when public exploit code is available","Business impact, data and regulatory exposure, and the attack's scope and stage","Asset count and the time since detection, so older incidents rise automatically"],"a":[2],"e":"Severity should reflect harm to the business: which systems and data are affected, regulatory exposure, and how far the attack has progressed. Host count and alert volume rewarded the noisy adware. CVSS rates a vulnerability, not an incident. Aging helps stalled cases but does not rank impact."},
{"id":"o44","d":6,"q":"A customer database runs on a managed cloud data platform. The web app connects through one shared service account, and a handful of data analysts also query it directly with personal accounts. The SOC wants to detect unusual access, such as bulk export of customer records. The platform supports query history and access logs. The company also uses a CASB for SaaS. Which data source is MOST valuable?","o":["Network flow logs from the database subnet, alerting on large outbound transfers to the internet","Database query and access logs, plus application logs that tie each request to an end user","Cloud IAM sign-in logs for the analysts, with alerts for logins from unusual locations","Endpoint DLP on analyst laptops, alerting when customer data is copied to removable media"],"a":[1],"e":"Query logs show who read what, how much, and when, and because the app uses a shared account, app logs are needed to map that activity to real users. Flow logs see volume but not which data. IAM sign-ins show logins, not data access. Endpoint DLP covers one exfil path but not access through the app."},
{"id":"o45","d":6,"q":"A SOC opens an incident for every IDS alert. Each incident triggers a legal review workflow, and counsel now gets over 200 requests a month, most for blocked scans. Counsel has started ignoring them, and a real data exposure notice sat unread for a week. The IDS was replaced last year with a newer model, and the SOC is planning to add a second shift in the spring. What is the BEST fix?","o":["Remove legal from the workflow and have the SOC decide when counsel should be informed","Raise IDS alert thresholds so fewer alerts reach the SOC and fewer incidents are opened each month","Batch legal notices into a weekly digest so counsel can review them in a single session","Define criteria for when an event becomes an incident, and trigger legal on those"],"a":[3],"e":"Events are observable occurrences, and only those that threaten or violate policy should become incidents and trigger response steps like legal review. Clear classification cuts noise while keeping legal involved when it counts. Removing legal loses needed oversight. Raising IDS thresholds hides activity. A weekly digest delays urgent notices."},
{"id":"o46","d":6,"q":"Ransomware encrypted a hospital's EHR database, and clinicians have been on paper records for three days. Immutable backups exist at 1, 7, and 30 days old. The investigation so far found persistence through a GPO-deployed scheduled task and a compromised backup admin account, but initial access is not yet dated. The EHR vendor is on site, and the board is pressing for service by tomorrow. Which recovery approach is BEST?","o":["Restore the 30-day backup, since it is the one most likely to predate the attacker and carry no persistence","Restore into an isolated environment, remove known persistence, reset credentials, reconnect in stages","Restore the 1-day backup to production now and put enhanced EDR monitoring on the restored servers","Rebuild the EHR servers from scratch and import the latest data exports provided by the EHR vendor"],"a":[1],"e":"With access not dated, any backup could carry persistence, so restoring into isolation, checking for the known mechanisms, and resetting compromised credentials before staged reconnection limits reinfection while still moving fast. The 30-day backup loses a month of clinical data and may still be infected. Restoring straight to production risks immediate reinfection through the GPO and backup account. A full rebuild is slower than the clinical need allows."},
{"id":"o47","d":6,"q":"A company uses Entra ID with smart lockout enabled. Threat intel says a group is spraying tenants through rotating residential proxies, trying one or two common passwords per account every few hours. The SOC has sign-in logs in the SIEM with IP, ASN, user agent, and result codes. Conditional access blocks legacy authentication. Which correlation pattern BEST detects this activity?","o":["Many failed attempts against one account from several IPs in a short time window","Many failures from one source IP across many accounts within five minutes","Low failure counts on many accounts over hours, linked by agent or ASN","Successful sign-ins from countries where the user has not signed in before"],"a":[2],"e":"Low-and-slow spraying through rotating proxies spreads a few failures over many accounts and IPs, so linking them by shared traits such as user agent or ASN over a longer window is the reliable signal. Many failures on one account is brute force. A single-IP rule catches crude spraying but misses rotating proxies. New-country sign-ins catch some successes but not the spray itself."},
{"id":"o48","d":6,"q":"A company uses Box as its corporate file platform. Many employees also have personal Box and Google Drive accounts. TLS inspection is enabled on the secure web gateway, except for a few banking and health sites. The company has a CASB that can run inline or through APIs. Leadership wants to detect and stop data leaving through personal cloud storage without breaking corporate Box. Which combination is MOST effective?","o":["Block the Box and Google Drive domains at the gateway and allow exceptions on request","Use API-mode CASB to scan the corporate Box tenant for sensitive files shared externally","Use inline tenant restrictions to allow only corporate instances, plus DLP and upload volume alerts","Use endpoint DLP to block uploads of confidential-labeled files to web storage from managed laptops, with alerts"],"a":[2],"e":"Inline instance awareness tells corporate from personal tenants on the same domain, so personal uploads can be blocked while corporate Box works, and DLP plus volume alerts catch misuse. Blocking the domains breaks corporate Box. API-mode CASB sees only the corporate tenant. Endpoint DLP depends on labeling and misses unlabeled data."},
{"id":"o49","d":6,"q":"All security tools, including the EDR console, SIEM, firewall managers, and cloud consoles, use SSO through a cloud IdP with push MFA. A tabletop explored an IdP outage combined with an attacker who has admin rights in the IdP tenant. Security admins realized they could not reach their own tools to respond. The IdP contract renews in eight months. Which control BEST addresses this?","o":["Federate a second IdP as a failover provider for all of the security tools in use","Keep offline copies of SIEM data so that investigations can continue","Set break-glass accounts outside the IdP with hardware MFA and alerts on use","Extend admin session timeouts so active sessions survive a short IdP outage"],"a":[2],"e":"Break-glass accounts that do not depend on the IdP, protected with hardware MFA and monitored for use, let responders reach their tools when the IdP is down or compromised. A second federated IdP adds complexity and can be affected by the same compromise path. Offline SIEM data helps analysis but not response. Longer sessions only help briefly and weaken security."},
{"id":"o50","d":6,"q":"A company's CMDB is about 60% accurate. Vulnerability scans keep missing servers, and an acquisition last year added subnets nobody has fully mapped. EDR covers most laptops but few servers. Cloud accounts are created by business units. Last month an unknown, unpatched server was used for initial access. The CMDB vendor just released a new UI. Which approach BEST improves the asset inventory for security operations?","o":["Make CMDB updates mandatory in change management and audit compliance quarterly","Run a full network discovery scan each quarter, including the acquired subnets","Continuously reconcile CMDB, EDR, cloud, network scan, and IdP data","Require new assets to register through NAC before they receive an IP address"],"a":[2],"e":"No single source is complete, so continuously combining CMDB, EDR, cloud, network, and identity data finds unknown assets and those missing controls. Mandatory CMDB updates help but rely on people. Quarterly scans leave long gaps and miss cloud. NAC registration helps on campus networks but not cloud or existing gaps."},
{"id":"o51","d":6,"q":"The SOC sees a Cobalt Strike beacon on a sales rep's laptop. The rep is working from a customer site on guest Wi-Fi, and the company does not use always-on VPN. The laptop runs a cloud-managed EDR and is enrolled in Intune. The rep is about to join a deal meeting and has not answered calls. The laptop's last full scan was two days ago. Which action is MOST effective right now?","o":["Disable the rep's account in the IdP and revoke sessions so the attacker cannot use the identity","Use EDR network containment to cut all traffic but the EDR cloud, then investigate remotely","Push a restrictive firewall profile through Intune and wait for the device to check in and apply","Keep calling the rep and ask them to disconnect from the Wi-Fi and power down the laptop for pickup"],"a":[1],"e":"EDR containment works on any network and leaves the investigation channel open, so it stops the beacon immediately. Disabling the account helps but the beacon still runs on the device. Intune policy may take time to apply. Calling the rep is slow and powering down loses memory evidence."},
{"id":"o52","d":6,"q":"During a large breach, the CISO is taking calls from reporters, the board, regulators, and key customers while also directing technical containment. Decisions on both fronts are stalling, and the IR team waits hours for answers. The company has a PR agency on retainer. The incident started in a cloud workload that was recently migrated. What is the BEST structural fix?","o":["Stand up an exec-led crisis team for strategy and communications, with IR reporting into it","Bring in the PR agency to handle all external calls and route only technical questions to the CISO","Name a deputy CISO to run communications while the CISO keeps leading technical containment","Add more responders from the IR retainer so containment moves faster with less CISO input"],"a":[0],"e":"Crisis management handles business decisions, legal, and communications, while IR handles the technical response. Separating them, with IR feeding the crisis team, unblocks both. A PR agency helps with messaging but cannot make business decisions. A deputy CISO still leaves security owning company-level decisions. More responders do not fix the decision bottleneck."},
{"id":"o53","d":6,"q":"A company already runs a SIEM with 15 log sources and three years of retention for compliance. Its EDR vendor is pitching XDR and says the SIEM could be retired. XDR would cover endpoint, identity, and email from that vendor plus a few third-party connectors. The SOC is small and struggles to pivot between consoles. Which statement BEST describes XDR's value here?","o":["It can retire the SIEM, since XDR ingests the same log types and meets compliance retention","Its main value is lower licensing cost through consolidating several tools with one vendor","Correlated detection and response across its integrated controls, alongside the SIEM","Its value is mostly better endpoint detection than a standalone EDR gives"],"a":[2],"e":"XDR's main benefit is correlating signals across endpoint, identity, and email and acting from one place, which helps a small SOC. It usually does not match a SIEM's broad third-party ingestion and long retention, so the SIEM likely stays for compliance. Cost savings may happen but are not the primary benefit. The endpoint engine is often the same as the EDR."},
{"id":"o54","d":6,"q":"A company is buying cyber insurance. The insurer requires use of a firm from its approved IR panel and will not cover fees for other firms without prior approval. The company already has a retainer with a different IR firm that knows its environment well. Legal wants IR work done under privilege. The retainer renews in four months. How should the architect handle this in the IR plan?","o":["Keep the current firm as primary and notify the insurer after the incident to seek reimbursement","Cancel the existing retainer and move fully to the panel firm to stay within the policy terms","Let the insurer choose the firm at the time of the incident so coverage stays intact","Build the panel firm into the plan, seek approval for the current firm, and set notice steps"],"a":[3],"e":"The plan should name the panel firm with pre-agreed engagement steps, try to get the existing firm approved, and spell out insurer notice timing and legal engagement for privilege. Notifying afterward risks denied coverage. Cancelling loses a firm that knows the environment, maybe needlessly. Deciding during the incident wastes critical time."},
{"id":"o55","d":6,"q":"Auditors flagged 40 domain admins and a shared root password on Linux servers. The company has budget for one privileged access project this year. The SOC wants both fewer standing privileges and better detection of misuse. Admins worry about slowing down urgent fixes. The Linux servers run a mix of RHEL and Ubuntu. Which control gives the BEST mix of prevention and detection?","o":["SIEM alerts on logons by privileged groups, with daily review by the SOC","Quarterly access reviews of privileged groups with removal of stale members","PAM with vaulting, just-in-time access, session recording, and risky-command alerts","Phishing-resistant MFA on all domain admin and Linux root logon paths"],"a":[2],"e":"PAM cuts standing privilege through vaulting and just-in-time access, and session recording and command alerts give detection, while emergency workflows keep urgent fixes moving. SIEM alerts detect but do not prevent. Access reviews are periodic cleanup. MFA protects logon but does not limit standing privilege or show what admins do."},
{"id":"o56","d":6,"q":"A researcher reports a flaw in the firmware of the company's industrial controllers and plans to publish in 90 days. A fix is possible in 60 days, but customers usually need six months to deploy firmware to plants. Legal suggests sending the researcher a cease-and-desist letter. The company has no PSIRT, and product security is handled by the engineering lead. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Send the legal letter to delay publication until most customers have had time to deploy the fix","Publish an advisory now so customers can apply network mitigations before details leak","Ship the fix in 60 days and let the researcher publish on day 90 as originally planned","Explain the field constraint, ask for an extension, and prepare mitigations and an advisory"],"a":[3],"e":"Coordinated disclosure works best when the vendor engages openly, explains why deployment takes longer, negotiates timing, and gives customers mitigations, with a coordinator such as CISA or CERT/CC if needed. Legal threats damage trust and often speed publication. Publishing now without a fix may help attackers. Accepting day 90 leaves most plants exposed with full details public."},
{"id":"o57","d":6,"q":"A company moved most workloads to SaaS and cloud over three years. Its SIEM still has the same 600 rules written for the on-premises environment, and leadership believes coverage is fine because alert volume is stable. The SIEM vendor offers a cloud content pack. The SOC also recently replaced its ticketing tool. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Buy the vendor's cloud content pack and enable its rules across all tenants","Migrate to a cloud-native SIEM with built-in connectors for SaaS platforms","Map current assets and threats to log sources and rules to find coverage gaps","Deploy a CSPM tool to find misconfigurations across the cloud accounts and SaaS"],"a":[2],"e":"Stable alert volume can hide the fact that old rules watch sources that barely matter now. Mapping today's assets and threats to what is collected and detected shows the real gaps first. A content pack or new SIEM may follow but is premature. CSPM finds misconfigurations, not detection gaps."},
{"id":"o58","d":6,"q":"Active Directory is fully compromised. IR shows initial access about 60 days ago, with domain admin rights soon after. DC backups go back 180 days. The DCs are virtualized, and the hypervisor admin account was also compromised. The company runs 400 apps that rely on AD. Leadership wants a plan that restores trust without an 18-month migration. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Reset all passwords and the krbtgt account twice in place, then monitor closely for attacker activity","Restore the newest DC backup to production and remove the persistence that IR has identified","Build a new forest and migrate users and applications to it over the coming year and a half","Run a documented forest recovery from a pre-compromise backup on clean, isolated infrastructure"],"a":[3],"e":"A forest recovery from a backup taken before the compromise, built on hypervisors the attacker never touched, restores trust with far less disruption than a new forest. In-place resets leave unknown persistence. The newest backup carries the attacker's changes. A new forest is valid but is the long migration leadership wants to avoid."},
{"id":"o59","d":6,"q":"A CISO wants a SOC maturity assessment to justify budget and to benchmark progress year over year. The company reports enterprise risk using the NIST Cybersecurity Framework. The SOC has 12 analysts, an MDR partner, and its own detection engineering. The board also asked about ISO 27001 certification. Which approach is MOST appropriate for the SOC assessment?","o":["Use NIST CSF implementation tiers alone, since they are already used at the enterprise level","Pursue ISO 27001 certification and use the audit results as the SOC maturity measure","Use SOC-CMM for a detailed SOC assessment, mapping results to the CSF for reporting","Measure ATT&CK detection coverage each year and report the percentage as maturity"],"a":[2],"e":"SOC-CMM is built to assess SOC people, process, technology, and services in detail, and mapping its results to CSF keeps board reporting consistent. CSF tiers are too high-level for SOC work. ISO 27001 certifies an ISMS, not SOC maturity. ATT&CK coverage is one technical measure, not overall maturity."},
{"id":"o60","d":6,"q":"A company runs production in one cloud region across three availability zones. Backups use the provider's native service and are stored in the same account and region. The backup service supports cross-region and cross-account copies. The CFO wants to limit added cost. The company also uses a separate SaaS tool for endpoint backup. Which change addresses the MOST significant risk?","o":["Spread the backup vaults across all three availability zones in the current region","Copy backups to another region in a separate account with immutable locks","Turn on object versioning for backup storage so deleted copies can be restored","Increase backup frequency from daily to hourly to reduce potential data loss"],"a":[1],"e":"With production and backups in one account and region, a regional outage or account compromise can take out both. Cross-region, cross-account, immutable copies address both threats. Spreading across zones does not help with regional or account events. Versioning in the same account can be undone by an admin attacker. More frequent backups improve RPO but not survivability."},
{"id":"o61","d":6,"q":"About 200 development teams each own their AWS accounts. The cloud workload protection platform sends roughly 3,000 alerts a week, all to the SOC, which lacks the context to judge most of them. Tickets the SOC forwards to dev teams often sit for weeks. Most alerts are posture findings, but a few are runtime threats like reverse shells. The platform also generates SBOM reports. Which operating model is MOST effective?","o":["Keep all alerts in the SOC and add two cloud specialists to handle the volume with better context","Send all alerts directly to the owning dev teams, who know their workloads and can fix issues quickly","Route posture findings to owners with SLAs; the SOC keeps runtime threats and escalates misses","Suppress posture findings in the SOC queue and alert only on runtime threats to reduce noise for now"],"a":[2],"e":"Owners are best placed to fix posture issues, while runtime threats need SOC response. Routing by type, with SLAs and SOC escalation, gives both speed and accountability. Keeping everything in the SOC does not scale. Sending runtime threats to dev teams delays incident response. Suppressing posture findings drops real risk."},
{"id":"o62","d":6,"q":"Analysts confirm an attacker exploited a zero-day in the company's VPN appliance. No patch exists yet, but the vendor has published a mitigation and an integrity check tool. About 8,000 remote staff depend on the VPN daily. The appliance also terminates a few site-to-site tunnels. Security leadership is debating options. What should the response architecture support?","o":["Shut the VPN down until a patch ships, and move remote staff to a virtual desktop service","Factory reset the appliance and restore its saved configuration to remove the attacker","Apply the mitigation, keep appliance evidence, hunt downstream, and reset credentials that passed through it","Replace the appliance with another vendor's product that is not affected by this vulnerability"],"a":[2],"e":"With no patch, the right path is to reduce exposure with the vendor mitigation, keep evidence, look for follow-on activity in the network, and reset credentials that passed through the device. Shutting down hits 8,000 users and may not be needed. A factory reset destroys evidence and may not remove persistence. A vendor swap is a later decision, not incident response."},
{"id":"o63","d":6,"q":"A payments web app handles about 50,000 requests per second. Logging budget allows only selected events to be sent to the SIEM, and PCI DSS applies. Developers currently log full request bodies at debug level in a few services. The app runs on Kubernetes with a service mesh, and the SIEM contract renews next spring. Which log events are MOST important to send for security monitoring?","o":["Full request and response bodies for transactions, so investigators can replay activity","All HTTP access logs at debug detail, including headers, from each service and the mesh","Authentication and authorization events, input failures, privilege changes, and sensitive data access","Only server errors and unhandled exceptions, since attacks often cause abnormal app behavior"],"a":[2],"e":"Authentication, authorization, input validation failures, privilege changes, and sensitive data access, with user and session context, cover most attacks at manageable volume. Full bodies add PII and cardholder data to logs and cost. Debug access logs are too much for the budget. Errors alone miss successful abuse."},
{"id":"o64","d":6,"q":"A peer in the company's ISAC shares an indicator set marked TLP:AMBER+STRICT. The SOC uses an MSSP for night monitoring, and an analyst wants to load the indicators into the MSSP's platform. The indicators relate to an active campaign. The ISAC uses STIX and TAXII for sharing, and the company's TIP supports marking-based distribution rules. The MSSP contract includes a confidentiality clause. What should the sharing process do?","o":["Share with the MSSP, since it works as part of the company's security function under contract","Strip the TLP marking and share only the raw IP and domain indicators with the MSSP","Ask the source for permission before sharing with the MSSP, and enforce markings in the TIP","Hold the indicators for 30 days and then treat them as TLP:GREEN for wider distribution"],"a":[2],"e":"AMBER+STRICT limits sharing to the recipient organization only, so the MSSP needs the source's permission, and the TIP should enforce markings. Plain AMBER might allow sharing with service providers, but STRICT does not. Stripping markings breaks trust and the protocol. TLP markings do not expire on a timer."},
{"id":"o65","d":6,"q":"A lab instrument controller runs Windows 7 with FDA-validated software that cannot be changed without revalidation. Replacement is planned in 18 months. The controller exports results to a file share nightly. The instrument vendor says EDR agents are not supported. The lab manager also asked for a new printer on that network. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Buy extended security updates for Windows 7 from Microsoft to keep it patched until replaced","Install a lightweight EDR agent anyway and accept the support risk for the validated system","Air-gap the controller and move results by USB drive so it has no network exposure at all","Segment it, allow only the export flow, monitor, and formally accept the risk until replacement"],"a":[3],"e":"Isolation, allowing only the export flow, monitoring, and time-bound risk acceptance tied to the replacement date reduce exposure without breaking validation. Windows 7 extended updates have ended. An unsupported agent may break the validated state. USB transfer adds a malware path and manual work."},
{"id":"o66","d":6,"q":"A global logistics company has three regional SOCs with different SIEMs, playbooks, and severity definitions. During a recent multi-region incident, each SOC rated the same activity differently and nobody coordinated for 18 hours. A SIEM consolidation is proposed for two years from now. The regions are in different time zones and languages. What is the BEST first improvement?","o":["Consolidate onto a single SIEM so the three SOCs share one data set and one common rule base","Merge the three regional SOCs into one global SOC with a single management chain","Hold a daily sync call between regional SOC leads to compare open incidents and ratings","Adopt a common taxonomy and severity model, a global incident commander, and shared cases"],"a":[3],"e":"The failure was in shared language and coordination, so a common taxonomy and severity model, a defined global escalation path, and shared case management fix it quickly. A single SIEM is a long project and does not fix process. Merging SOCs is a big change and loses regional coverage. A daily call is too slow for an active incident."},
{"id":"o67","d":6,"q":"An attacker exfiltrated a customer database containing EU and California residents' personal data. The IR lead wants to finish forensic scoping before involving anyone outside security, estimating a week. The company has a DPO and outside privacy counsel. The database was migrated to a new platform last month. Which activity should happen EARLY, in parallel with containment?","o":["Finish scoping first so legal receives complete facts before notification decisions","Notify all customers now so they can protect themselves while the investigation proceeds","Send regulators a preliminary report today to show good faith before scoping is done","Engage legal and the DPO to assess notification duties and deadlines as facts develop"],"a":[3],"e":"GDPR's 72-hour clock and state laws start from awareness, so legal and privacy must assess duties as the investigation runs. Waiting a week risks missed deadlines. Notifying all customers before knowing scope causes confusion. Whether and what to tell regulators is a legal decision, not a reflex."},
{"id":"o68","d":6,"q":"A company wants to use canary files to catch ransomware on its file servers. The servers hold about 40 TB across thousands of shares. Backup jobs and antivirus scan the shares nightly. A pilot placed obvious decoy files and got false alerts every night. The SOC has SOAR with EDR integration. Ransomware groups seen in the sector usually encrypt shares in directory order. Which design is MOST effective?","o":["Put a single hidden folder of canaries at the root of each server to keep users away from them","Mark the canary files read-only so that ransomware cannot encrypt them and they stay intact","Name the files clearly as canaries so that staff know not to open, move, or change them","Seed realistic files where ransomware hits first, exclude scanners, auto-contain on change"],"a":[3],"e":"Canaries work when they look real, sit where ransomware enumerates early, are excluded from legitimate scanners that cause false alerts, and trigger automated containment. One hidden folder may be hit late. Read-only files may be skipped or still give no signal. Clearly labeled canaries can be spotted and avoided by attackers."},
{"id":"o69","d":6,"q":"A company's DR plan was written five years ago. Since then, half of its systems moved to cloud and several key processes now rely on SaaS. The plan still lists on-premises servers and a tape vendor the company dropped. The auditor wants an updated plan within six months. The DR site lease expires in two years, and the CIO would like to close it if possible. What should be done FIRST?","o":["Run a full DR test now to see which parts of the current plan still work in practice","Buy a cloud DR service that replicates current workloads and provides runbooks","Rewrite the plan using the cloud provider's DR templates for the migrated workloads","Redo the BIA and dependency mapping across on-premises, cloud, and SaaS services"],"a":[3],"e":"The plan reflects an environment that no longer exists, so updated impact analysis and dependency mapping must come first to set priorities and targets. Testing an outdated plan mostly confirms it fails. Buying a service or using templates before knowing requirements and dependencies risks a plan that misses SaaS and cross-system links."},
{"id":"o70","d":6,"q":"A SOC collects from about 150 sources, each with its own parser and field names. Analysts write a separate query for each firewall and EDR vendor, and cross-source hunting is slow. The company is adding a security data lake alongside its SIEM. Some vendors support CEF output, many do not. The SOC is also hiring two engineers. Which approach is MOST useful?","o":["Require all sources to send logs in CEF and drop sources that cannot meet the requirement","Keep raw formats and build query-time views per source for analysts to use","Normalize to a common schema like OCSF in the pipeline, keeping the raw events as well","Store only raw logs in the data lake and rely on natural-language search to find events"],"a":[2],"e":"Normalizing to an open schema like OCSF or ECS in the pipeline gives consistent fields across the SIEM and data lake, and keeping raw data supports forensics and reparsing. Forcing CEF drops useful sources. Query-time views per source keep the per-vendor work analysts already struggle with. Raw-only storage leaves normalization unsolved."},
{"id":"o71","d":6,"q":"An e-commerce company is under a volumetric DDoS that its scrubbing provider is handling. At the same time, the SOC sees successful logins to the admin portal from new IPs using two service accounts. Most of the IR team is on the DDoS bridge call. The admin portal sits behind the same CDN. The CEO is asking for hourly updates on the outage. What should the IR lead do?","o":["Focus the team on the DDoS until service is restored, and then review the admin portal logins","Block all access to the admin portal until the DDoS ends and then investigate the logins","Geo-block the regions where the new admin IPs come from and keep the team on the DDoS","Treat the DDoS as a possible diversion and assign separate staff to the logins right away"],"a":[3],"e":"Attackers use DDoS to distract responders. Successful logins with service accounts may be the real attack, so they need separate, immediate investigation while the provider handles the DDoS. Waiting gives the attacker time. Blocking the portal outright may disrupt operations and does not answer what happened. Geo-blocking is easy for attackers to dodge."},
{"id":"o72","d":6,"q":"A SOC includes offshore MSSP analysts who work in the company's SIEM. Logs contain EU personal data such as names, emails, and IP addresses. The DPO is concerned about access and transfers. Investigations sometimes need to identify the real user. The SIEM supports field-level controls. The MSSP's contract includes standard contractual clauses for transfers. Which control is MOST appropriate?","o":["Strip personal data from logs at ingestion so the SIEM holds no personal data that needs protecting","Limit SIEM access to internal staff and move the MSSP to alert-only notifications","Use role-based access with masked personal fields, approved unmasking, and query auditing","Encrypt the SIEM data at rest and in transit so personal data stays protected throughout"],"a":[2],"e":"Role-based access with masked fields lets MSSP analysts work while limiting exposure, and controlled unmasking plus audit covers the cases that need real identity. Stripping data at ingestion breaks investigations. Pushing the MSSP out loses its value. Encryption protects storage and transit but not what users can see."},
{"id":"o73","d":6,"q":"During a major outage, recovery staff opened the DR plan and found 40 applications, all labeled critical. With no order to follow, they started with the systems whose owners called loudest, and payroll came up before the identity service it depends on. The plan was signed off by every business unit. The DR site had enough capacity. What is the root problem?","o":["The DR site cannot restore all 40 applications in parallel within the time allowed","Recovery runbooks lack technical detail on the steps for each individual application","The BIA did not tier applications by impact and map the dependencies between them","The DR team has not practiced the plan through regular structured exercises"],"a":[2],"e":"Without a BIA that ranks applications by impact and maps dependencies, there is no recovery order, which is why payroll came up before identity. Capacity was fine. Runbook detail and training matter but do not set priorities."},
{"id":"o74","d":6,"q":"A company wants better detection of lateral movement in its Windows domain. Budget allows one major data source improvement this year. Today the SIEM gets firewall logs and domain controller security logs, but nothing from member servers or workstations. Attackers in a past incident used PsExec and WMI. The company also runs a NetFlow collector. Which data source is MOST valuable to add?","o":["NetFlow from the core switches, with alerts on new SMB and RPC flows between hosts","More detailed domain controller logging, including all Kerberos ticket requests","Endpoint process creation with command lines, remote logons, and EDR telemetry","Firewall logs from the internal segmentation firewalls between server VLANs"],"a":[2],"e":"Lateral movement shows on the hosts: remote logons and process creation with command lines reveal PsExec, WMI, and similar tools. NetFlow shows connections but not what ran. DC logs see authentication but miss execution. Internal firewall logs show allowed flows, not actions."},
{"id":"o75","d":6,"q":"The patch tool reports 99% compliance across 4,000 servers. An attacker still exploited a server marked patched, because a reboot was pending and the vulnerable code was still loaded. Audit wants a more reliable measure. The patch tool vendor offers a new dashboard module. Servers run Windows and Linux, and about 300 are in a newly acquired business unit. Which approach is MOST reliable?","o":["Use the patch tool's own compliance report, adding a pending-reboot column","Require server owners to attest monthly that patches are installed and active","Verify independently with authenticated scans or version checks against inventory","Monitor patch approval status in WSUS and in the Linux package repositories each week"],"a":[2],"e":"Independent checks of what is actually running, compared against the full inventory, catch pending reboots, failures, and missing assets. The patch tool reports its own view and may miss unmanaged servers. Attestation is manual. Approval status shows intent, not installed state."},
{"id":"o76","d":6,"q":"A company uses one MSSP for all monitoring. The MSSP has standing admin access to the EDR console and firewall managers through shared accounts, plus a site-to-site VPN into the management network. This morning the MSSP disclosed a breach of its own environment, with few details. The contract runs two more years. The company's internal SOC has two analysts. What should the company do FIRST?","o":["Give notice to end the contract and begin moving monitoring to another provider","Wait for the MSSP's full breach report so that actions are based on confirmed facts","Suspend MSSP access paths, rotate the shared credentials, and review MSSP-originated actions in the logs","Bring all monitoring in-house right away using the two internal analysts and existing tooling"],"a":[2],"e":"The immediate risk is the MSSP's trusted access being used against the company, so cut those paths, rotate shared credentials, and check what was done through them. Ending the contract is a later decision. Waiting for a report leaves access open. Two analysts cannot take over full monitoring overnight, and it does not close the access risk."},
{"id":"o77","d":6,"q":"Attackers used a malicious OAuth app connected to the company's Salesforce org to pull customer records. The IR team found that detailed event logs were not available because the Event Monitoring add-on was never licensed, and nobody knew how to reach Salesforce security. The IR plan covers on-premises and IaaS only. The company uses SSO for Salesforce. What should the updated plan include for SaaS incidents?","o":["A contract clause requiring each SaaS provider to run incident response on the company's own behalf","Provider escalation contacts, known logs and response actions, and log export set up in advance","An API-mode CASB connected to major SaaS apps to scan for sensitive data and risky sharing","Reliance on SSO and IdP sign-in logs as the main evidence source for SaaS investigations"],"a":[1],"e":"Under shared responsibility, the customer must know how to reach the provider, what logs and actions exist (and license them), and export logs before an incident. Providers rarely run customer IR. A CASB helps posture and detection but is not an IR plan. SSO logs miss OAuth app activity, which bypasses interactive sign-in."},
{"id":"o78","d":6,"q":"A company's cloud bill rose 40% in a week. It runs Kubernetes clusters with autoscaling and a few GPU instance families for an ML team. GuardDuty is enabled. The finance team already gets billing alerts, but those arrive days after usage. The company also runs a cloud cost optimization tool, and the ML team often launches large jobs without notice. Which signal is MOST indicative of cryptojacking?","o":["Billing anomaly alerts showing spend in the compute category running well above the forecast","High sustained CPU on cluster nodes, since mining is compute-heavy work by design","New compute in unused regions or types, sustained load, and connections to mining pools","An increase in failed API calls from service accounts across the cloud accounts"],"a":[2],"e":"Mining shows as unexpected compute, often in unused regions or GPU types, running hot and talking to mining pools, which GuardDuty and flow logs can show. Billing alerts lag by days. High CPU alone is normal with autoscaling and ML work. Failed API calls may show recon but not mining."},
{"id":"o79","d":6,"q":"A multi-tenant SaaS company has 300 enterprise customers, and many contracts include a right-to-audit clause. Requests for on-site audits are growing and pulling engineers off projects. The company has a SOC 2 Type II report and ISO 27001 certification. All customers share the same infrastructure, and the sales team wants to offer audits as a premium feature. Which approach BEST supports these clauses?","o":["Give auditors read-only access to the SIEM so they can verify controls directly","Decline on-site audits and refer customers to the SOC 2 report as the only evidence","Offer standard evidence packages and a trust portal, with scoped audits under NDA","Run a separate on-site audit for each customer that asks, scheduled across the year"],"a":[2],"e":"Standardized evidence, attestations, and a trust portal meet most audit needs without exposing other tenants, and scoped audits handle the rest. SIEM access exposes other customers' data. Flatly declining may breach contracts. Individual on-site audits do not scale."},
{"id":"o80","d":6,"q":"A company is acquiring a smaller firm and plans to connect the networks in 60 days. The target has an MSP-run firewall and EDR but no SOC, and reports no known incidents. The deal team wants assurance that the target is not already compromised. The target recently passed a PCI assessment, and its CEO says an outside review would be redundant. Which activity is MOST appropriate before connecting?","o":["Run authenticated vulnerability scans across the target's networks and fix critical gaps","Commission a penetration test of the target's perimeter and internal networks","Do a compromise assessment using threat hunting and forensic sweeps of the target","Review the MSP's EDR alert history and firewall logs for the past twelve months"],"a":[2],"e":"A compromise assessment looks for evidence of current or past intrusion, which is the question here. Vulnerability scans and pentests find weaknesses, not existing attackers. Reviewing MSP alerts only shows what the tools already caught. A PCI pass says little about compromise."},
{"id":"o81","d":6,"q":"Rumors of a data breach are spreading on social media. The investigation has confirmed unauthorized access but not yet what data was taken. The CEO wants to deny the breach until forensics finish. Legal prefers saying nothing. Customer support is getting hundreds of calls. The company recently changed PR agencies. Which principle should guide communications?","o":["Deny the breach until forensics confirm the scope, so the company does not overstate it","Say nothing publicly until all facts are known, to limit legal exposure and speculation","Share accurate, approved, consistent updates that fit legal duties, without overclaiming","Publish full technical details right away to show transparency and rebuild customer trust"],"a":[2],"e":"Communications should be accurate and timely, approved through a set process, consistent across channels, and aligned with legal obligations, saying what is known without speculating. A denial that turns out false destroys trust. Silence while support is flooded feeds rumors. Full technical details can help attackers and create legal risk."},
{"id":"o82","d":6,"q":"Recent incidents in the company's sector involved attackers phishing SIEM and EDR administrators, then disabling detections and deleting alerts before deploying ransomware. At this company, security admins use their regular accounts with push MFA to manage security tools. The SIEM is SaaS-hosted. The SOC is expanding to a second shift. Which design change MOST reduces this risk?","o":["Put the SIEM and EDR consoles behind the corporate VPN so that only internal users can reach them","Back up SIEM configuration and detection rules daily so they can be restored after tampering","Use separate admin identities with phishing-resistant MFA, PAWs, and out-of-band config alerts","Move from the SaaS SIEM to a self-hosted SIEM inside the data center to limit exposure"],"a":[2],"e":"Attackers who target security tools first go after the admins, so dedicated admin identities, phishing-resistant MFA, privileged workstations, and alerts on tool changes sent outside the tool cut the risk. A VPN does not stop a phished admin. Backups help recovery but not detection. Self-hosting does not change the admin attack path."},
{"id":"o83","d":6,"q":"A new financial regulation requires reporting significant incidents within 4 hours of classifying them as significant. The company has no written criteria for significance, so teams have debated classification for up to a day. Legal must approve all regulatory filings. The SOC runs 24x7 with an MDR partner. Which design element BEST supports compliance?","o":["Auto-submit reports to the regulator whenever the SIEM rates an incident as critical in severity","Keep a 24x7 legal on-call rotation so filings can be approved around the clock","Treat all high-severity incidents as significant and report each one to be safe","Set significance criteria, decision trees, templates, pre-assigned approvers, and clocks"],"a":[3],"e":"Clear criteria end the classification debate, and templates, named approvers, and clock tracking let the team file within 4 hours. Auto-submission removes legal judgment. Legal on-call helps approvals but not classification. Over-reporting floods the regulator and wastes effort."},
{"id":"o84","d":6,"q":"A company has about 2,000 open exceptions to its vulnerability remediation SLAs. Most were approved by the security team, have no end date, and list no compensating controls. Some app owners now ask for exceptions by default. Internal audit flagged the backlog. The GRC tool supports workflow. The CIO wants the backlog cut in half by year end without slowing releases. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Stop granting new exceptions and require all findings to be fixed within the standard SLA","Keep security as the approver but require a written justification for each request","Auto-approve exceptions for legacy systems, since those cannot be patched in practice","Require risk-owner sign-off, compensating controls, expiry dates, and periodic review"],"a":[3],"e":"Exceptions are risk acceptance, so the business risk owner should sign off, with compensating controls, an end date, and review. Banning exceptions ignores real constraints and pushes risk underground. Security approving alone keeps risk with the wrong owner. Auto-approving legacy systems hides risk."},
{"id":"o85","d":6,"q":"EDR shows an active command-and-control beacon on the CFO's laptop. The CFO is traveling and refuses isolation before a board presentation in three hours. Policy says the SOC should isolate high-severity infections, but the CFO outranks the CISO. The laptop holds draft earnings data. The help desk can courier a loaner by tomorrow. What should the incident handler do?","o":["Isolate the laptop now as policy requires and inform the CFO of the reason afterward","Wait until after the presentation, then isolate, while watching the beacon closely","Escalate to the CISO and CEO with options, like blocking C2 or a clean device","Let the CFO's executive assistant decide the timing based on the CFO's calendar"],"a":[2],"e":"An executive refusing containment is a risk decision above the handler's authority, so escalate fast with clear options like blocking the C2 domain or giving a clean device for the presentation. Isolating against a senior executive without escalation may be overruled and damage trust. Waiting three hours with active C2 risks earnings data. An assistant has no authority to accept the risk."},
{"id":"o86","d":6,"q":"Production backups use the cloud provider's native service and sit in vaults in the production account. They are encrypted with a customer-managed key that also lives in the production account. Several admins have broad rights there. The provider supports vault lock in compliance mode and cross-account copies. The company plans a migration to a new region next year. Which improvement BEST protects backups against account compromise?","o":["Turn on vault lock in compliance mode for the vaults inside the production account","Copy backups into a locked vault in another account using keys that account owns","Add an IAM policy denying backup deletion to all roles except the backup admin role","Require MFA for backup deletion on the vaults and alert on deletion attempts by admins"],"a":[1],"e":"Even locked backups are useless if an attacker in the account can schedule deletion of the encryption key. A separate account with its own keys and a locked vault protects against both deletion and key loss. Same-account vault lock leaves the key exposed. IAM deny policies and MFA can be changed by an admin who controls the account."},
{"id":"o87","d":6,"q":"The board asks for a quarterly security operations dashboard. The SOC currently reports alert counts, tickets closed, and tool uptime. Board members say the numbers do not tell them whether the company is safer. The company recently had a breach that went unnoticed for 40 days. Reports will be prepared by the GRC team. Which set of metrics is MOST appropriate?","o":["Total alerts, tickets closed, and analyst headcount, trended quarter over quarter","Security tool uptime, log ingestion volume, and the number of integrated sources","Time to detect and contain, critical exposure trends, and risk vs. appetite","Number of attacks blocked by the firewall, email gateway, and EDR each quarter"],"a":[2],"e":"Boards need outcome and risk metrics: how fast attacks are caught and stopped, whether critical exposure is shrinking, and where risk stands against appetite. Alert and ticket counts measure activity. Uptime and ingestion are operational health. Blocked attack counts look reassuring but say little about missed attacks like the 40-day breach."},
{"id":"o88","d":6,"q":"IR playbooks assume the SOC can reach laptops over the VPN to collect data and push fixes. Most staff now work remotely and use the VPN only for a few legacy apps. During a recent incident, the SOC could not reach the affected laptop for two days. Devices run cloud-managed EDR and Intune. Some users are in countries with poor connectivity. What should be updated?","o":["Require a persistent full-tunnel VPN on laptops so the existing playbooks work as they were written","Add steps to have the user ship the laptop to IT when remote access is not possible","Rewrite playbooks around EDR, Intune, and identity actions that work over the internet","Add a step for the help desk to call the user and talk them through manual collection"],"a":[2],"e":"The tools already in place work over any internet connection, so the playbooks should use EDR collection and containment, MDM actions, and identity controls. Always-on VPN is possible but adds cost and friction just to fit old playbooks, and struggles on poor links. Shipping laptops takes days. Talking users through collection is slow and unreliable."},
{"id":"o89","d":6,"q":"The last five post-incident reviews produced 60 action items. Twelve are done, and the same root cause, unmonitored service accounts, showed up in three incidents. Action items live in the review documents. The security team uses Jira for other work. The CISO reports to the CIO. Which approach is MOST effective at turning lessons into real improvements?","o":["Hold more frequent review meetings so action items stay visible to the teams involved","Hire a project manager to chase action owners and send weekly status emails","Add an appendix to each review listing all open actions from earlier reviews","Track actions with owners and dates, report to the risk committee, and verify fixes"],"a":[3],"e":"Lessons turn into change when each action has an owner and due date in a tracked system, status goes to a body with authority, and completion is verified rather than assumed. More meetings and appendices keep actions visible but not owned. A project manager helps chase but lacks authority and verification."},
{"id":"o90","d":6,"q":"A company rolled out an approved enterprise AI assistant with agents that can call internal APIs to create tickets and query HR data. Usage logs show some staff still paste customer data into consumer chatbots. The security team has a secure web gateway with DLP and an API gateway in front of internal services. Legal is drafting an AI use policy. Which risk is MOST important to monitor?","o":["Harmful or biased output from the approved assistant, flagged through user feedback","License and seat usage of the assistant to confirm adoption against the business case","Data sent to unapproved AI services, and agent API calls that break from baseline","Prompt volume per user, to find staff relying on AI tools more than expected"],"a":[2],"e":"The biggest security risks are sensitive data leaving through unapproved services and approved agents being abused to act on internal systems, both visible through DLP and API logs. Output quality matters but is not the main security concern. License and volume tracking are adoption metrics."}
);
